{
  "info": {
    "name": "Player & Server Moderation Session",
    "description": "Every REST endpoint of both services, run top to bottom as one story: two players register and become friends, a team is formed, the team opens a shift, a junior mod joins and is given records and channels, one applicant is judged, the shift ends, and the SessionCompleted event turns the result into XP. Ids and tokens are carried from one request to the next automatically, so nothing has to be copied by hand.\n\nEverything goes through the Gateway. A client sends Authorization: Bearer <token> and the Gateway validates it, drops the header and tells the service who is calling through X-Player-Id, X-Session-Id, X-Session-Role and X-Record-Access. No request here sets those headers: the Gateway refuses to believe a caller who names itself, which the Gateway boundary folder checks.\n\nTwo base URLs, because a request's privileges come from the door it arrived at. gatewayBaseUrl is the published port, http://127.0.0.1:8080 from your machine. internalBaseUrl is http://gateway:8001, which is not published: it serves the endpoints the contract marks internal, the ones a service calls and a client may not. Reaching it means running inside the Docker network:\n\n    docker run --rm --network pad-team-20_backend -v \"$PWD/postman:/etc/newman\" \\\n      postman/newman run player-and-session.postman_collection.json \\\n      --env-var gatewayBaseUrl=http://gateway:8000\n\nRun it from your machine instead and the story still runs, but the five internal requests cannot reach gateway:8001 and fail, along with the assertions downstream of them: scoring needs an outcome reported, and progression needs SessionCompleted delivered.\n\nWS /sessions/{id}/live cannot be part of a collection export. It is the one place a token is still checked by the service, because the client connects to it directly: POST /ws/negotiate at the Gateway returns the URL, ws://127.0.0.1:3011/sessions/<sessionId>/live, and the token goes in ?token= or Authorization. POST /internal/events/session-completed on Player is not in the communication contract either; it stands in for the RabbitMQ subscription until the broker is part of the stack.",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
  "variable": [
    {
      "key": "gatewayBaseUrl",
      "value": "http://127.0.0.1:8080"
    },
    {
      "key": "internalBaseUrl",
      "value": "http://gateway:8001"
    }
  ],
